    {
      "id": "formal-semantics-properties-proved",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The formal semantics' step function is proved deterministic, fires at most one transition per behavior per step, and terminates every run under the step limit, with no unproved assumption in the proofs.",
      "rationale": "Supported by the test that builds formal/ (WG-4685): lake build type-checks every theorem of formal/Exegra/Properties.lean (determinism of step and of runs, at most one fired transition per behavior per step, every run stops within the step bound, a dropped event fires nothing, a failed step keeps nothing), and its #guard_msgs checks fail the build if any theorem depends on sorryAx or on an axiom other than propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. The test also refuses sorry, admit and axiom declarations in the sources. Exegra records the build as test evidence; it does not check the proofs itself.",
      "subjects": [
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "invariants-proved-unbounded",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A state-invariant claim reported as verified by proof has a Lean theorem, building with no unproved assumption, that the invariant holds in every reachable configuration of its scenario under its fault model.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4686). The bounded part exists: invariants-proved-within-bounds (WG-4696) covers every configuration within a stated bound, with its own evidence. Proving an invariant for every reachable configuration at every depth, by induction over the step relation, remains WG-4686, so this claim stays not evaluated until evidence of that exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "proof-export",
