      }
    },
    {
      "id": "poisson-arrivals-by-inversion",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Poisson arrivals, constant or ramped, take exactly one arrival draw per instant, computed by inverting the integrated rate with exact integer square roots and rounding to the nearest base unit; no valid input is refused or fails for rejected candidates, and a draw whose instant falls at or after its phase end or the horizon creates nothing.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Workloads). Negative twin: a ramp from 0 to 10^7/s over 1000 s in unit ns, which would need about 1.4 x 10^5 rejections under thinning, must run with one draw per instant; an implementation that rounds interarrivals down instead of to the nearest unit must fail the fixture that measures the arrival rate of a constant phase at a mean interarrival of 100 base units. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "overlapping-faults-follow-active-set",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "With windowed faults that overlap on one resource, the value in effect at every fault-edge is the declared function of the active set: the last active set-capacity, set-class-limit or replace-demand in the order of the input's faults list, the product of the active scale-demand factors reduced to lowest terms, and collapsed while any collapse-classes is active; demand and scale are evaluated at the grant against the faults active then; when a collapse ends, waiting requests return to their own classes in (since, seq) order; and under a collapse each request keeps its class for maxWait and metrics.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Faults in format 2). Negative twin: two overlapping set-capacity faults whose order in the input is swapped must give different capacities during the overlap; an engine mutant that applies the most recently started fault instead of the last in input order, or that leaves requests in the collapsed queue after the collapse ends, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "broadcasts-evaluated-at-delivery",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A broadcast reaches the instances that are up when it is delivered (keyed instances created since its creation and instances restarted since included) and skips down ones without an ignored entry; targeted entries keep the instance and incarnation fixed when they were created; routed entries are decided, with their counters, draws and incarnation, when first taken as head, and a delayed copy keeps the decision; and the next arrival instant of a workload is created exactly once, even when a fault delays or drops the first entry of the current instant.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664, WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Instances and Workloads). Negative twin: a broadcast at time 10 to a behavior whose instance crashed at 5 and restarted at 8 must reach the restarted instance while a reply created at 7 for its old incarnation is dropped; an arrival created at 6 and routed least-busy, delivered at 9, must see the load at 9 and must not be stale; a delayed first arrival must not create the next instant twice. Engine mutants that route at creation or recreate the instant fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "request-lifecycle-defines-outcomes",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Every request of a class follows the request lifecycle of docs/simulation.md: it starts when its start entry is first taken as head; it ends once, at the first delivered end event with its key (a deliver step that does not fail, whether or not a transition fires), as error at once when its start entry is dropped, crashed, stale or handled by no transition, or as timeout on a closed-client timeout; it is censored otherwise; only ok requests are finite samples; and every sample count, bucket power and derived metric is computed from these outcomes.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Request lifecycle). Negative twin: a fixture whose start event is dropped by a drop-event fault, whose start event reaches a behavior with no transition for it, and whose start is routed to a crashed instance must record three error ends at the step that lost them, and a p99 claim over it must count them as +infinity; a metrics implementation that leaves lost starts out of the samples, or ends a request on a dropped end event, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "temporal-claims-checked-on-all-runs",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A never, precedes or eventually claim reported by exegra prove as holding has a Lean theorem, accepted by Lean, that the verified explorer found the form true on every complete run of its scenario (every order of simultaneous events, every subset of its faults, each run ending at quiescence, at the step limit or in a failed step); a reported counterexample is a real run that violates it, and a path the bound or the state budget cuts off is reported incomplete.",
      "rationale": "WG-4698. The forms are defined in formal/Exegra/Temporal.lean with the meaning checkTrace gives them; tcheck_sound and tcheck_counterexample_real are proved with only Lean's standard axioms, pinned with #guard_msgs. The test runs exegra prove on tests/fixtures/race: an eventually that holds, an eventually that fails because a path drops the event and ends first, a precedes that holds, and a precedes that the scripted run satisfies and a reordering violates. Each result is discharged by native_decide, so it also trusts the Lean compiler. Exegra records the test as evidence; it does not check the proofs itself.",
      "subjects": [
        "exhaustive-explorer",
        "proof-export"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "register-import-holds-every-line",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The register importer writes nothing unless the Markdown file rebuilds line for line from the generated sections with every entity marker expanded to the lines it came from, and every imported table row rendered from its properties gives the cells of its source row.",
      "rationale": "A converted specification replaces its Markdown, so a line the conversion dropped or a cell it changed would be lost silently. The two checks make loss a failed import instead.",
      "subjects": [
        "register-importer"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
